          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Asthma with an acute exacerbation: is that one code, or a code plus something else?",
          "answer": "**Short answer:** The retrieved coding material suggests asthma is classified along two separate axes, so the answer may involve more than one code. It does not give the exact codes for asthma with an acute exacerbation, so I can't confirm whether that case needs one code or a code plus an additional one.\n\n**What the sources say:**\n\n- Asthma sits in category J45 in Chapter 10 of ICD-10-CM. The coding summary on J45 says asthma questions \"so often reduce to two lookups rather than one,\" but the passage I received was cut off before it named those two lookups. (Source: *Coding asthma: J45 and its severity and exacerbation axes*.)\n- Exacerbation and status asthmaticus are mutually exclusive. A record documenting status asthmaticus is coded to that, not also to acute exacerbation. Which one applies is a documentation question for the clinician's record, not a judgment the coder makes. (Same source.)\n- If severity is not documented, asthma goes to an \"unspecified\" code. Severity cannot be inferred from treatment, peak-flow readings, or other parts of the chart. (Same source.)\n- The general \"with\" convention, which presumes a link between two linked conditions unless the record says otherwise, is described for type 2 diabetes. I did not find a passage confirming that it applies to asthma. (*Coding type 2 diabetes: E11*.)\n\n**What I could not confirm:** The code lookup returned only chapter-level results and nearby categories, such as acute bronchitis (J20 to J22) and chronic lower respiratory diseases (J40 to J47). It did not return the specific J45 codes for an acute exacerbation, so I won't name one.\n\nFor the exact code and any required additional code, check the official ICD-10-CM tabular list for category J45 or ask a certified medical coder. They work from the actual clinical documentation, which determines the correct code.\n\nIf you are having an asthma attack now, with trouble breathing, lips or face turning blue or grey, or medicine not helping, seek emergency care immediately.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
